//
// UPSTREAM BUG PROBE — loft-lang/loft#739. A keyed lookup on a store bound with `store_persist_bind`
// PANICS the runtime. ⚠ `--native` ONLY — the interpreter answers correctly.
//
//   thread '<unnamed>' panicked at src/database/search.rs:229:37:
//   find called on non-collection type: <varies> (db=128)
//
// ⚠ The type it names DIFFERS from run to run and program to program (`Pixel`, `short<-32768,false>`,
// `Block`), which is itself part of the report: the message prints whatever type currently holds id 128,
// so it points at something unrelated to the code that failed. That is what made it expensive — the first
// message blamed the imaging library in a program whose failing line indexed a road tile.
//
// It matters because keyed access is the point of a bound store. ITERATION works and is the workaround
// `tools/gen_heights.loft` uses (the loop variable is a live view, so writes through it reach the file);
// a lookup is what you reach for first.
//
//   loft --native --lib lib tools/loft_store_key_probe.loft <block.store>
//
// It must be a POPULATED, EXISTING store — binding a fresh empty one and inserting hits a different panic
// (src/database/structures.rs:80), and an empty store answers a lookup with null and no abort.
//
// ⚠ POINT IT AT A COPY, and the path is REQUIRED for that reason — it used to default to the committed
// Enschede fixture and quietly damaged it. `store_persist_bind` REWRITES THE `.dschema` SIDECAR to include
// the binding program's own wrapper struct (`Block size=4 struct{roads@0:hash<TTile[tkey]>}`) and changes
// the schema hash with it. loft#705 gates `store_load` on that hash, so merely *reading* a shipped block
// with a tool that binds it can leave the block unloadable by the app. `tools/loft_bug_gate.sh` copies
// first for exactly this reason.
#cwd

use routing_kernel::(TTile, TRoad, TStep);

struct Block { roads: hash<TTile[tkey]> }

fn main() {
  a: vector<text> = [];
  for x in arguments() { a += [x]; }
  path = a[0] ?? "";
  if path == "" { println("#P SKIP usage: loft_store_key_probe <block.store>  — point it at a COPY"); return; }
  if !file(path).exists() { println("#P SKIP no block at {path}"); return; }

  b = Block { roads: [] };
  ok = store_persist_bind(b.roads, path);
  n = 0;
  key = 0;
  for t in b.roads { n += 1; if key == 0 { key = t.tkey; } }
  println("#P bound={ok} tiles={n} first key={key}");
  if n == 0 { println("#P SKIP the store is empty — the lookup only aborts on a populated one"); return; }

  println("#P about to look that key up — this is the line that aborts");
  t2 = b.roads[key];
  println("#P FIXED — lookup returned null={t2 == null}, no panic");
}
